00:05:28.301 --> 00:05:31.620
- All right, let's dive
in by starting today

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.630
with testosterone.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:36.090
Super popular subject.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.610
Lots and lots of growth in
testosterone replacement

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:42.968
right now, because of the
fact that testosterone

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:45.580
decreases as we age,

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:48.047
and that's disconcerting
to a lot of people.

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.320
- Oh, for sure.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.422
After the age of 30 to 40, we lose about

00:05:51.422 --> 00:05:54.520
1% of our testosterone
levels, men and women,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.860
it's important for both sexes.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.110
But the market has grown.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.160
So TRT or testosterone replacement therapy

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.820
has grown from a few
hundred million 10 years ago

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.557
to multi-billion dollars now.

00:06:04.557 --> 00:06:07.711
And it's widely used
not just to supplement

00:06:07.711 --> 00:06:10.120
for sexual dysfunction,

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.120
which is a common side
effect of low testosterone,

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.064
but for other things that
include mental health,

00:06:15.064 --> 00:06:18.750
building muscle, overall
fitness, vitality.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:19.839
- And people swear by this.

00:06:19.839 --> 00:06:22.010
I mean, a lot of people did, they do this,

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.150
they say they feel younger,

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:24.723
they say they feel more vigorous.

00:06:24.723 --> 00:06:26.690
They want to have sex again.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.510
They want to have sex all the time.

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.570
There's an important
distinction to make here though,

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.030
which is just because you feel younger,

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:34.190
does not necessarily mean

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:37.286
you are reversing your biological aging.

00:06:37.286 --> 00:06:38.170
- Right.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:39.880
Let's make a clear distinction.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.090
We've talked about adversity mimetics

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.400
and the last episode, we
talked about the supplements

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.244
and the kind of things that
you can do in your life

00:06:46.244 --> 00:06:47.350
to mimic that.

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.510
We want our bodies to
be in a state of fear.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:50.720
That times are tough,

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.566
and they need to hunker down and survive.

00:06:52.566 --> 00:06:54.300
There are other types of things

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.340
that we're going to talk about,

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.147
which are abundance mimetics.

00:06:57.147 --> 00:06:58.656
Testosterone is one,

00:06:58.656 --> 00:07:00.632
we're going to get to
growth hormone as well.

00:07:00.632 --> 00:07:02.484
These tell the body that times a good,

00:07:02.484 --> 00:07:06.197
to build your muscle, to
reproduce, go forth, multiply.

00:07:06.197 --> 00:07:08.020
- And that's what testosterone is doing.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:09.791
We get testosterone replacement therapy,

00:07:09.791 --> 00:07:13.273
we are putting a substance
back into our body

00:07:13.273 --> 00:07:16.550
that has been reduced,
but which signals our body

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.760
that times are good enough,
it's okay to build muscle,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.546
it's okay to run around
chasing wooly mammoths,

00:07:21.546 --> 00:07:23.899
chasing the cave women around.

00:07:23.899 --> 00:07:24.732
- Yes.

00:07:24.732 --> 00:07:25.977
That for sure.

00:07:25.977 --> 00:07:28.755
And that benefited our
ancestors very well,

00:07:28.755 --> 00:07:31.200
but it didn't help them long-term.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.750
What we now are appreciate
is the body has cycles

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.780
of want and need and adversity,

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:37.907
which is good for the
long-term health of the body,

00:07:37.907 --> 00:07:40.883
and times where things are
abundant, times to grow,

00:07:40.883 --> 00:07:43.611
reproduce, which are great short-term,

00:07:43.611 --> 00:07:46.120
but probably not great
for long-term health

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.260
and longevity either.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.700
- Okay, so let's talk
about what actually happens

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:54.120
when our testosterone lowers
and what happens importantly

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.480
when we put it back.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:56.833
Because this is helping a lot of people

00:07:56.833 --> 00:08:01.833
with a lot of problems that
are coincidal with aging.

00:08:02.033 --> 00:08:02.990
- Oh, for sure.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.938
There've been a lot of
studies, millions of dollars,

00:08:04.938 --> 00:08:08.410
and many thousands of
people treated with TRT

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:09.316
as it's called.

00:08:09.316 --> 00:08:12.560
Shelly Basin, who's a
colleague of mine at Harvard

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:13.881
has done a lot of these studies,

00:08:13.881 --> 00:08:16.400
and he's found some improvements,
short term improvements,

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.330
so of course improvements in libido,

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.580
but also he shows a
dose dependent increase

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.244
in skeletal muscle mass,
so you get bigger muscles,

00:08:23.244 --> 00:08:26.300
you got more power, you can climb stairs,

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:28.640
you can walk further in six minutes,

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.540
improves aerobic capacity.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.589
These are all good things that he sees,

00:08:31.589 --> 00:08:34.110
but actually it's not all good news.

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:35.260
There were some side effects

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.720
and may actually impact longevity.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:41.616
So some of the risks of TRT,
there's worst sleep apnea,

00:08:41.616 --> 00:08:44.420
you can actually get larger breasts.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.130
A little lower fact is your testosterone

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:47.480
can be turned into estrogen,

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:50.424
which is a risk for men particularly.

00:08:50.424 --> 00:08:53.580
You can have what's called
benign prostatic hyperplasia.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.250
So bigger prostate, need to
go to the bathroom at night,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.201
shrinking testicles, not something I think

00:08:58.201 --> 00:08:59.643
many of us would want,

00:08:59.643 --> 00:09:01.870
and increasing red blood cell protection,

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.350
which could lead to blood clots.

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:05.650
- And if you're already
predisposed to prostate cancer,

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:07.110
you have cancerous growth already.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.974
This could actually aggravate the tumors.

00:09:08.974 --> 00:09:09.842
- Yes.

00:09:09.842 --> 00:09:12.080
And actually the opposite is true for you.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.434
If you look at the history
of eunuchs, they live longer.

00:09:14.434 --> 00:09:16.550
And so it may actually
be that supplementing

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:20.255
with testosterone is not
conducive to longer life.

00:09:20.255 --> 00:09:22.480
- So this is an important principle here,

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.170
and we're going to come back
to this a couple of times

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.710
on this episode, which is just because

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.240
something makes you feel good today,

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.340
and just because it might
even be medically beneficial

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:35.430
to you today, that does
not necessarily mean

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:40.030
it is addressing the
causes of biological aging.

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:42.450
It maybe fixing something
that you have a problem with,

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:43.950
it may be improving even your quality

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:47.376
of life right now long-term,
that's not the same thing

00:09:47.376 --> 00:09:51.690
as reducing or eliminating biological age.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.150
- Yeah, let's go back to
the main concept here today,

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:58.870
which is that adversity
mimetics are great,

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.240
things that mimic lack of
food and a lot of exercise,

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.885
but these abundance mimetics

00:10:04.885 --> 00:10:07.740
are probably a problem long-term

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.010
even though they make you
feel great short term.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:10.882
- Okay.

00:10:10.882 --> 00:10:12.710
And that's sort of giving
away the punchline for HGH,

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.287
but we need to talk about HGH as well.

00:10:14.287 --> 00:10:17.560
Human growth hormone, also something

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.092
that is really popular right now,

00:10:19.092 --> 00:10:23.080
people supplementing
with human growth hormone

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:27.318
to address things like
decreased exercise capacity,

00:10:27.318 --> 00:10:31.690
decreased bone density,
decreased muscle mass,

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.060
and increased body fat.

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:35.342
This works, right, for those things.

00:10:35.342 --> 00:10:37.734
HGH is helpful.

00:10:37.734 --> 00:10:39.160
- Well, no question.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:41.920
Short-term, you'll feel
better, you'll grow muscle,

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:44.320
you'll have less fat on
growth hormone therapy.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.360
The science says that this may not be good

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:48.563
for long-term health and wellness.

00:10:48.563 --> 00:10:51.110
Going back to the original
studies of Cynthia Kenyon,

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:52.850
which we talked about in episode one,

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:55.614
those mutant worms had low
levels of growth hormone

00:10:55.614 --> 00:10:58.220
and insulin like growth hormone signaling.

00:10:58.220 --> 00:10:59.310
- And they live longer.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:00.500
- Twice as long.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.760
And this has been reproduced also in mice.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.070
And even in humans, there
are the Loren dwarfs,

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:05.903
we were talking about earlier.

00:11:05.903 --> 00:11:08.220
These are smaller people
that have low levels

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.467
of growth hormone, or actually IGF-1,

00:11:10.467 --> 00:11:12.860
one of the versions of this pathway,

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.060
they are relatively
resistant against cancer

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.283
and heart disease, and
may even live longer.

00:11:17.283 --> 00:11:19.450
Conversely, if you're supplementing

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:20.677
with growth hormone long-term,

00:11:20.677 --> 00:11:23.170
the chances that you're
having the opposite.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.190
That you feel great, but long-term,

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:27.190
you might have even greater risk of cancer

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:28.023
and heart disease.

00:11:28.023 --> 00:11:31.560
- And you know people who are
on HGH for various reasons.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:34.490
One of the immediate side effects,

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:35.830
even before we get to the sort of like

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:39.230
the longevity implications is sore joints,

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:40.356
carpal tunnel syndrome.

00:11:40.356 --> 00:11:41.189
- Right.

00:11:41.189 --> 00:11:43.180
This is the kind of thing
that that does happen.

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:44.830
Now, I just want to be clear.

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:47.170
There are some benefits
to bringing the levels

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:48.740
back up to normal, okay?

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.340
But going to high,
which a lot of people do

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:52.536
if they want additional benefits,

00:11:52.536 --> 00:11:55.030
that's where I think that
the main risk comes from.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:59.520
- Another risk immediate is
increased insulin resistance.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:00.353
- That's true.

00:12:00.353 --> 00:12:01.410
We get high levels of blood sugar

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.500
or we get high cholesterol,
carpal tunnel, edema,

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:07.040
so buildup of fluid, joint pain.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:10.949
- Okay, so soreness in your
joints, insulin resistance,

00:12:10.949 --> 00:12:13.537
another one is an
increase in the likelihood

00:12:13.537 --> 00:12:17.060
of type two diabetes because
of that insulin resistance.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:20.720
All of this actually kind of
sounds like symptoms of aging.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:21.553
- Right.

00:12:21.553 --> 00:12:23.630
And that's the risk, that the
animal studies are telling us

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:25.979
that growth hormone will tell
your body that times are good,

00:12:25.979 --> 00:12:28.900
abundance mimetic, and that's not good

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.690
for long-term effects of health,

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.258
and probably what's going on
is some acceleration of aging.

00:12:33.258 --> 00:12:36.696
- Okay, this isn't to say
again, I think this really,

00:12:36.696 --> 00:12:39.970
we don't want to say human
growth hormone is bad.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.280
Testosterone is bad.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:45.400
People need to have a
naturally healthy level

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.320
of these hormones in their bodies.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:48.536
- There are ways to do that naturally.

00:12:48.536 --> 00:12:51.870
So with testosterone, you can
work out the bigger muscles

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.390
in your body and that also
help with growth hormone.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:57.180
So I work out my thighs
and my back particularly,

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:58.950
keep those nice and strong,

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:00.532
and I've found my natural
testosterone levels

00:13:00.532 --> 00:13:03.450
go up to normal levels with that.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:04.770
So that's the way to do it.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.910
With growth hormone, it's all about eating

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:08.140
at the right time and sleeping.

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:11.300
So if you eat not too close to sleep time,

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:12.890
and then you rest through the night,

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.150
and have a good night's sleep,

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:17.150
that's the best way to improve
your growth hormone levels.

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:18.183
- And if you're doing all of that

00:13:18.183 --> 00:13:20.810
and you're still struggling
to keep these levels up,

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:22.786
that's the time to talk to your doctor

00:13:22.786 --> 00:13:24.430
about supplementation,

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.515
but that supplementation needs to be aimed

00:13:26.515 --> 00:13:30.026
not at a hyper increasing
the levels of these

00:13:30.026 --> 00:13:32.520
in your bodies, but just keeping them

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:35.038
at that sort of like that
natural healthy level.

00:13:35.038 --> 00:13:36.140
- Well, exactly.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.445
But I would encourage everyone
to try it naturally first

00:13:38.445 --> 00:13:40.740
- So so far, this sort
of feels like bad news,

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:42.110
'cause these are the
things that a lot of people

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:43.080
have gotten into their heads

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.745
that might be beneficial to anti-aging,

00:13:45.745 --> 00:13:49.330
and what you're saying and
what the evidence suggests

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:51.590
is that there is no anti-aging benefit,

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:53.410
there might be a short term gain,

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.936
not a long-term anti-aging benefit,

00:13:55.936 --> 00:13:57.929
but these aren't the only things

00:13:57.929 --> 00:14:01.980
that are sort of like hot
on the market right now.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:04.070
Another thing that's really
popular to talk about,

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:07.380
really popular to try, where it is legal,

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:09.857
is peptide supplementation.

00:14:09.857 --> 00:14:10.690
- Yeah.

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:12.660
So peptides, just like
hormones, are made of

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:14.730
strings of amino acids,
but typically smaller.

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:16.310
About a hundred amino acids.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:17.850
You can synthesize them on a machine

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:19.447
or extract them from tissues,

00:14:19.447 --> 00:14:22.520
and they're used by cells to
communicate between each other.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.049
So your brain will put out
peptides to signal the body,

00:14:25.049 --> 00:14:27.117
your liver, and your muscle,

00:14:27.117 --> 00:14:29.020
and some of these are really good.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:29.933
Some are really bad.

00:14:29.933 --> 00:14:32.370
We don't know what they all do.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.797
There are probably a
thousand made by the body.

00:14:34.797 --> 00:14:37.790
We've probably only know
about what 20 of them do

00:14:37.790 --> 00:14:38.922
in great detail.

00:14:38.922 --> 00:14:41.380
- What we do know is, as a class,

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.681
they help cells
communicate to one another.

00:14:43.681 --> 00:14:44.560
- Right.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:47.780
- And so just sort of like
holistically speaking,

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:49.030
it's like, oh, that's really good,

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:52.994
because in part aging is a
loss of cellular communication,

00:14:52.994 --> 00:14:56.938
and so peptides help increase
that cellular communication.

00:14:56.938 --> 00:14:59.843
Voila, they must be great for aging.

00:14:59.843 --> 00:15:03.730
- Well, again, it's whether
you're in adversity mimetic,

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:05.010
or an abundance mimetic.

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:05.843
That's the difference.

00:15:05.843 --> 00:15:07.440
And really you have to make a distinction

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.696
between those two things.

00:15:08.696 --> 00:15:12.425
One of the best studied
peptides of all time is insulin,

00:15:12.425 --> 00:15:15.066
and clearly that's important
if you're deficient in it.

00:15:15.066 --> 00:15:17.275
We've known this since the 1920s.

00:15:17.275 --> 00:15:20.310
- We actually have a hundred
years now, it was in 1921,

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.695
which was when insulin
was first discovered.

00:15:22.695 --> 00:15:23.528
- Right.

00:15:23.528 --> 00:15:26.430
Banting and Best was 1921,
found they could extract

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:29.994
this insulin extract from
the pancreas of a dog

00:15:29.994 --> 00:15:32.564
and injected into a dog that had diabetes

00:15:32.564 --> 00:15:34.900
and cure it within hours.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:37.630
- And within months, they were
trying this on human beings,

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:38.917
because that's how
things worked back then.

00:15:38.917 --> 00:15:41.230
- Yeah, and they even
won Nobel Prizes quicker.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:42.800
It was only one or two years afterwards

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:44.180
that they won the Nobel prize for that.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:46.897
- So they went from this works in dogs

00:15:46.897 --> 00:15:50.055
to let's try this out in a 14 year old boy

00:15:50.055 --> 00:15:53.210
to Nobel prize in the
span of like two years.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:55.490
- Well, it was worth it,
because in those days,

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.240
particularly type one diabetics,

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:59.518
which is the childhood version of this,

00:15:59.518 --> 00:16:02.328
the lack of insulin meant
that they would have to go,

00:16:02.328 --> 00:16:04.290
the kids would have to
go on a starvation diet

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:06.542
and even then, they would
die within a year or so.

00:16:06.542 --> 00:16:07.560
It was horrific.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.520
And suddenly people
were living for decades.

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:12.100
That first patient lived
for another 13 years.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.595
- And so this set us down
the road of studying peptides

00:16:14.595 --> 00:16:16.748
and their health benefits,

00:16:16.748 --> 00:16:20.600
and then a really big thing
happened in the sixties,

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:23.423
which is we figured out
how to synthesize peptides.

00:16:23.423 --> 00:16:24.256
- Right.

00:16:24.256 --> 00:16:25.230
That was a big deal.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:28.084
And then in 1978, they started to be made

00:16:28.084 --> 00:16:30.150
using recombinant DNA technology.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:32.950
And that's where we find
ourselves today is that

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:35.420
most of these hormones and peptides

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:36.887
that are available on the market

00:16:36.887 --> 00:16:39.832
are made using recombinant DNA technology.

00:16:39.832 --> 00:16:42.550
Though these very small
