00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:53.929
to reduce fine lines,
wrinkles, and grow hair again.

00:21:53.929 --> 00:21:55.550
There's a whole bunch of-

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:59.830
- Is this based on, I mean,
is this anecdotal evidence?

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:01.220
Is this research-based evidence?

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:03.370
How do we know these things
about these different peptides?

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:05.280
- Yeah, most of these
come from mouse studies

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:07.016
and then they're anecdotal from studies

00:22:07.016 --> 00:22:09.716
that clinicians are currently doing.

00:22:09.716 --> 00:22:11.880
But yeah, again, I want to stress

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:14.910
that most of these things that
we're saying are anecdotal

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:17.850
and not yet scientifically validated.

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:19.306
- These are some people's experiences

00:22:19.306 --> 00:22:22.470
and this is all one of the reasons

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:25.360
why there is a pretty
significant movement right now,

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:27.966
this sort of like save
peptides movement of people

00:22:27.966 --> 00:22:30.297
who are contacting the FDA and saying,

00:22:30.297 --> 00:22:32.610
"please make these things
more available to us again."

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:34.070
- Well, yeah, there's one in particular,

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:36.580
thymus and alpha one, which has been known

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:39.159
since the 1970s to be
beneficial, it seems to be great,

00:22:39.159 --> 00:22:41.044
particularly for helping against

00:22:41.044 --> 00:22:43.745
fighting bacteria and viral infections,

00:22:43.745 --> 00:22:47.717
but it was also listed on the
banned substances in 2020,

00:22:47.717 --> 00:22:49.640
and there's a movement to bring it back,

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:52.760
because with COVID-19, it's
thought that it could help.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:53.843
Talking about other ones,

00:22:53.843 --> 00:22:56.230
some of these work with
fat loss apparently.

00:22:56.230 --> 00:22:58.970
We've got to some Tesamorelin CJC-1295

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:02.747
is taken with one called
Ipamorelin, there's AOD-9604,

00:23:02.747 --> 00:23:05.530
and the BP-157 again,

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:07.738
can be good for weight loss apparently.

00:23:07.738 --> 00:23:09.770
One of the other ones I mentioned humanin

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:12.390
is, again, one of those
mitochondrial derived peptides

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:14.440
like MOTS-C, and in mice at least,

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:16.890
has been shown to help
against type two diabetes,

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:18.940
high blood sugar, cardiovascular disease,

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:21.410
and even help with
neurodegenerative disease

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:22.243
like Alzheimer's.

00:23:22.243 --> 00:23:24.201
But in humans, it's not
clear if that's true.

00:23:24.201 --> 00:23:26.870
Pinealon is a really interesting one.

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:29.838
It's actually an active peptide complex

00:23:29.838 --> 00:23:34.480
and has been thought to treat
memory, attention deficits,

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:37.432
brain injury, stroke,
chronic fatigue, et cetera.

00:23:37.432 --> 00:23:39.460
But again, early days.

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:42.220
We don't know for sure if this
is actually true in humans.

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:44.090
And then finally, oh,
we should just mention,

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:46.430
there are others that are
used to combat fatigue

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:48.310
and also boost the immune system.

00:23:48.310 --> 00:23:52.901
They go by names like Dixhexa,
Cerebrolysin, Semax, Selank,

00:23:52.901 --> 00:23:55.697
or even BPC 157 is used with some Semax,

00:23:55.697 --> 00:23:57.310
and Cerebrolysin together.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.930
Again, these are all
pretty much experimental.

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:02.110
They've been shown to
be effective in mice,

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:03.160
but still we have a long way to go

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:05.487
before we know if they're
truly effective in humans.

00:24:05.487 --> 00:24:08.190
And there, we always
need placebo controlled,

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:09.920
double blind clinical studies.

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:11.280
- Which are underway.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:13.960
I mean, there's lots and lots
of clinical studies going on.

00:24:13.960 --> 00:24:16.030
It's just not moving as fast as I think

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:18.410
a lot of people would
really like it to move.

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:21.610
But that's the reality of the
space that we're in right now.

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:23.723
- Well, it is, and often it is frustrating

00:24:23.723 --> 00:24:25.960
and I can speak from personal
experience how long it takes

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:27.620
to make a drug can be extremely slow,

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:30.200
and we're all getting
older, we're not getting,

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:32.656
in the case of most of us,
we're not getting any younger,

00:24:32.656 --> 00:24:36.190
and there is a desire to rush into things,

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:38.900
but I think we need to
exercise some caution here,

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:40.500
because you can have these
negative side effects,

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:43.030
particularly in the case of
peptides, an immune reaction

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:44.890
they could be deleterious in the long run.

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:48.280
- And in that long run
though, you see these

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:51.425
as a very promising avenue
for a lot of therapies

00:24:51.425 --> 00:24:53.157
that are going to help a lot of people.

00:24:53.157 --> 00:24:55.590
Not just with individual conditions,

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:57.201
but you think with aging.

00:24:57.201 --> 00:24:58.210
- Oh, for sure.

00:24:58.210 --> 00:24:59.280
Not all of these, of course,

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:01.270
but those that mimic adversity.

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:02.580
MOTS-C is a good example of one

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:03.890
that I think has the right signature.

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:05.350
I think they have a lot of promise.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:08.750
- Because there is an
explosion of research

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:10.580
going on sort of at the
same time with peptides

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:12.520
and this other thing that
we're about to talk about,

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:14.458
exosomes, I think these
happened in the same breath.

00:25:14.458 --> 00:25:18.555
A lot of times people talk
about peptides and exosomes.

00:25:18.555 --> 00:25:20.837
Both of these are cellular signalers,

00:25:20.837 --> 00:25:23.247
but exosomes work, well,
they are different,

00:25:23.247 --> 00:25:24.850
and they work a little differently.

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:25.683
- Well, very different.

00:25:25.683 --> 00:25:27.740
These are packages of information.

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.020
They contain, well,
they're membrane bound,

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:31.166
so they're little vesicles,

00:25:31.166 --> 00:25:32.100
they're about the size of a virus,

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:34.722
so they travel between cells
and into cells very easily,

00:25:34.722 --> 00:25:36.840
and inside, it's not just one message.

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:38.000
There's multiple messages.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.320
There's peptides inside,
there's DNA, there's RNA,

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:43.030
even what are called micro RNAs,

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:45.602
which are similar to
peptides, very small pieces,

00:25:45.602 --> 00:25:47.761
in this case of nucleic acid material,

00:25:47.761 --> 00:25:50.900
that can go from the
brain to other tissues

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:52.342
from tissues back up to the brain.

00:25:52.342 --> 00:25:55.530
And this is essentially the
way the body can coordinate.

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:58.700
So if there's an injury on your
hand, your brain will know,

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.280
or if your brain gets injured,
the rest of your body knows,

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:02.983
and you need to have these messengers

00:26:02.983 --> 00:26:05.890
to coordinate the system in the same way

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:07.990
you could imagine the US post.

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:10.980
And so these a little
mail deliveries of cargoes

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:13.483
that we can both intercept and read,

00:26:13.483 --> 00:26:16.510
perhaps we can use these
to diagnose diseases,

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:19.260
including cancer, but also
we can make more of them

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:21.900
and infuse them into people
to give a false alarm,

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:24.670
perhaps even, to simulate this adversity

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:26.090
and make us live longer.

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:28.550
- And before we get to
those therapeutic processes,

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:32.852
one of the really fast moving avenues

00:26:32.852 --> 00:26:37.670
for using exosomes is
basically collecting them

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:39.118
from the blood, counting them up,

00:26:39.118 --> 00:26:41.900
and being able to diagnose
different injuries.

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:46.250
And even like, you can pull
people's blood these exosomes,

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:49.400
and then you can say like,
"oh, they have an injury here.

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:50.730
They have a disease there."

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:51.563
- Right.

00:26:51.563 --> 00:26:52.700
Yeah, there are companies now that,

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:55.760
there's one called Zar
Therapeutics, there are others,

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:57.690
that you can read the cargo,

00:26:57.690 --> 00:26:59.860
and you can say, "okay, that
comes from the pancreas.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:03.888
That is a signature of a stage
three pancreatic cancer."

00:27:03.888 --> 00:27:05.950
So we may be able to use these

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:07.670
to diagnose cancer years in advance.

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:08.503
- Without biopsying-

00:27:08.503 --> 00:27:11.410
- Right, just a blood test or
even a finger prick at home,

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:13.100
send it off, and diagnose cancer.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:15.880
- And that would be reason
enough to be excited, I think,

00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:18.230
about the potential for exosomes

00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:20.050
to tell us what's going on in our bodies,

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:22.480
but what else we're learning
right now about exosomes

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:26.440
is that if we supplement
these into the bodies of mice,

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:28.640
like by injecting them into their,

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:31.740
back in to their bloodstreams,
it can help with injury.

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:32.573
- Yeah.

00:27:32.573 --> 00:27:34.010
I mean, there's a really
well-known paper now

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:37.040
from August 2017, by my good colleague

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:38.522
down at Albert Einstein
College of Medicine,

00:27:38.522 --> 00:27:41.690
Dong Shanghai, and his
expertise is looking

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:43.430
how the hypothalamus, the little organ

00:27:43.430 --> 00:27:44.700
at the base of the brain communicates

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:45.533
to the rest of the body,

00:27:45.533 --> 00:27:48.140
and he's found that
inflammation in and damage

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:52.240
to that part of the body
affects the rest of the animal,

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:53.073
including aging.

00:27:53.073 --> 00:27:55.120
And in this case, he found, interestingly,

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:56.960
that neuronal stem cells,

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.841
these progenitors in the hypothalamus,

00:27:58.841 --> 00:28:01.290
they secrete exosomes that tell the body

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:02.620
to hunker down and survive.

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:03.990
And the mouse can actually live longer

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:07.210
if you isolate these exosomes
and give it to the mouse.

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:10.330
They also did the opposite,
they deleted these special cells

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:13.280
that make the exosomes and
the mice got diseases of aging

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:14.400
and lived shorter.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:15.430
- Why is it happening?

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:16.840
- Well, we don't know.

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:18.520
In most cases, there's so many things

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:21.840
within the exosomes that we,
it's very hard to figure out

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:23.217
which of those particular cargoes

00:28:23.217 --> 00:28:25.670
is responsible for the biological effect,

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.500
but ultimately if we can figure that out,

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:29.922
we could make exorcisms with
just that particular component,

00:28:29.922 --> 00:28:32.370
and that could be a drug in the future.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:37.370
- There was another fairly
recent paper on exosomes,

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:41.180
2019 paper from some Chinese researchers

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:46.180
that used exosome replacement
to address ulcer healing,

00:28:46.355 --> 00:28:49.237
and what they concluded
was that the exosomes

00:28:49.237 --> 00:28:52.767
are actually rejuvenating senescent cells.

00:28:52.767 --> 00:28:54.970
Really fascinating here.

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:56.364
- Well that, so senescent cells,

00:28:56.364 --> 00:28:58.910
if you miss the previous
episode, our cells,

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:01.580
that as they get old and
they have ex-differentiation,

00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:04.400
they lose their ability to
read this their DNA correctly.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:05.800
They become senescent.

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:06.940
They stopped dividing.

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:08.550
They sit there, and they start secreting

00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:12.014
inflammatory molecules, can
even cause cancer to develop.

00:29:12.014 --> 00:29:14.800
We try to delete these cells.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:16.690
We know that in a mouse
and probably in a human,

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:19.040
if you get rid of these
inflammatory senescent cells,

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:20.195
it's good for health.

00:29:20.195 --> 00:29:22.660
But here we're saying, what
the researchers are saying,

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:24.463
is that these exosomes,

00:29:24.463 --> 00:29:27.428
a particular type of exosomes
can reverse senescence.

00:29:27.428 --> 00:29:28.720
That's unheard of.

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:29.970
If that turns out to be true,

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:31.786
that would be quite a remarkable thing,

00:29:31.786 --> 00:29:34.183
meaning one day we don't
have to kill off these cells,

00:29:34.183 --> 00:29:35.760
which in the brain,

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:37.400
you wouldn't want to do that necessarily,

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:38.710
we could actually rejuvenate them,

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:40.307
and get them to grow again, healthily.

00:29:40.307 --> 00:29:42.731
- Right now, the way we
understand how to do that

00:29:42.731 --> 00:29:45.295
is through the Yamanaka factors,

00:29:45.295 --> 00:29:47.451
but this would be another avenue

00:29:47.451 --> 00:29:52.250
toward that same goal of
taking exdifferentiated cells

00:29:52.250 --> 00:29:56.849
and sending them back into
a differentiated state.

00:29:56.849 --> 00:29:58.876
- Well, the Yamanaka factor,

00:29:58.876 --> 00:30:02.027
rejuvenation of senescent
cells is preliminary.

00:30:02.027 --> 00:30:04.360
This is some of the work
we're doing in my lab,

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:05.303
and it's not even clear
if the Yamanaka factors

00:30:05.303 --> 00:30:07.550
are pertinent enough
to reverse senescence,

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:09.356
but this finding that you referred to

00:30:09.356 --> 00:30:12.200
is the first instance at
that that might be possible.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:14.010
- All of this is why
there's a lot of excitement

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:17.720
around this particular potential therapy.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:19.840
But again, just like peptides,

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:21.780
this is sort of the wild west right now,

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:24.622
but people are already taking exosomes

00:30:24.622 --> 00:30:28.594
and having them shot into their bodies.

00:30:28.594 --> 00:30:32.054
- They can be delivered
by IV or intramuscularly,

00:30:32.054 --> 00:30:34.895
but again, we, we don't know
what the longterm benefits

00:30:34.895 --> 00:30:36.860
or side effect are.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:38.280
So we have to be careful.

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:41.200
It's still available in
some parts of the US,

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:42.033
but we don't know.

00:30:42.033 --> 00:30:42.866
- This is not one of those things

00:30:42.866 --> 00:30:45.780
you have to go to Costa Rica
for, at least as of right now,

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:47.090
the FDA hasn't cracked down,

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:49.500
they haven't said this is a step too far.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:50.333
- That's right.

00:30:50.333 --> 00:30:52.300
But while exosomes
aren't completely banned

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:53.950
in the United States,
you can bet that the FDA

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:56.550
is looking at this for a
potential safety concerns.

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.060
And we'll see how things
go over the next few years.

00:30:59.060 --> 00:31:01.681
- That's not the same thing as
saying, like, "go do it now!"

00:31:01.681 --> 00:31:04.083
- No, no, that's not what we're here for.

00:31:04.083 --> 00:31:07.880
oWe want to educate and look
at what might be available

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:09.220
down the line once things are proven

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:10.112
to be safe and effective.

00:31:10.112 --> 00:31:13.230
- And this is another one of
those things, like peptides,

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:17.340
you're feeling positive
that there is going to be

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:20.350
actual clinical research that will show us

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:21.810
the actual benefits of these.

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:24.470
We don't have to make
super, super guesses,

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:26.318
we can actually see in the research

00:31:26.318 --> 00:31:28.762
how exosomes are going to be beneficial

00:31:28.762 --> 00:31:33.762
and then start to implement
them into our longevity regimes

00:31:34.810 --> 00:31:35.860
in a responsible way.

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:36.693
- That's right.

00:31:36.693 --> 00:31:37.533
And just to be clear, exosomes are

00:31:37.533 --> 00:31:41.880
a highly validated scientific pursuit,

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:43.832
and there are biotech companies,
pharmaceutical companies,

00:31:43.832 --> 00:31:47.150
building giant factories,
billions of dollars,

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:49.363
to make exosomes, to treat diseases.

00:31:49.363 --> 00:31:51.880
The question is, do they
work for long-term health

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:53.350
and longevity and how safe they are

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:55.730
to be used over decades.

00:31:55.730 --> 00:31:57.010
That we don't know yet.

00:31:57.010 --> 00:32:00.160
- Okay, let's move than
into an area of research

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:01.878
that is even further along.

00:32:01.878 --> 00:32:05.575
Still, we're learning more
about every single day,

00:32:05.575 --> 00:32:10.575
but also has potential
anti-aging implications.

00:32:11.536 --> 00:32:13.130
Stem cells.

00:32:13.130 --> 00:32:14.680
Let's start with a little primer.

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:15.931
Stem cells are?

00:32:15.931 --> 00:32:19.610
- Stem cells are cells that
can divide asymmetrically

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:21.900
to produce cells that
go on to make tissues.

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:23.482
So for skin, you need stem cells to make

00:32:23.482 --> 00:32:26.120
all the skin that grows
over your lifetime,

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:28.670
and they retain youth so
that they can keep dividing

00:32:28.670 --> 00:32:30.990
over and over, and they don't become

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:33.610
any particular certain
cell type over time.

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:36.130
- And stem cells can
become more stem cells

00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:38.799
or they can become any kind of cell.

00:32:38.799 --> 00:32:40.730
- And there's a few different types.

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:41.590
- Right.

00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:43.150
- The two main classes are multipotent,

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:45.882
which can make a few
different types of tissues,

00:32:45.882 --> 00:32:49.110
these are cells that you
typically get from an embryo.

00:32:49.110 --> 00:32:51.790
We can go all the way back to age zero,

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:54.380
and what are called
pluripotent stem cells,

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:57.250
which can make any type of
cell in any type of tissues.

00:32:57.250 --> 00:32:58.690
- And you can think of this sort of like

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:00.470
as one of those trees, right?

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:03.350
Like you have the pluripotent
cell that can do anything,

00:33:03.350 --> 00:33:04.183
and then after a while,

00:33:04.183 --> 00:33:05.900
a little further down in the development,

00:33:05.900 --> 00:33:07.220
you have the multipotent cells,

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:11.660
which are still able to transition

00:33:11.660 --> 00:33:15.060
into several different kinds of cells,

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:17.134
but not all the different kinds of cells.

00:33:17.134 --> 00:33:18.020
- Exactly.

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:20.110
And so if you want to
build a mini human brain

00:33:20.110 --> 00:33:22.450
or rebuild a kidney out of a skin cell,

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:24.420
you have to wind the clock
all the way back to zero

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:26.186
and start again, and that's called

00:33:26.186 --> 00:33:29.100
inducing a pluripotent stem cell state.

00:33:29.100 --> 00:33:30.820
- And that goes back to what
we were talking about earlier,

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:32.543
the Yamanaka factors,
this is one of the ways

00:33:32.543 --> 00:33:37.080
that we've identified that
we can turn a stem cell

00:33:37.080 --> 00:33:39.260
or any kind of cell from being a stem cell

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:40.960
back into a state of pluripotency.

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:43.410
- Yeah, let's just talk
about Shinya Yamanaka,

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:45.230
because it's really
quite an amazing story.

00:33:45.230 --> 00:33:48.675
So Yamanaka in Japan,
in the early well, 2010s

00:33:48.675 --> 00:33:51.410
was, in his lab, was figuring
out or trying to figure out

00:33:51.410 --> 00:33:54.710
how do you take an adult cell
from an animal or a human

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:56.582
and make it pluripotent.

00:33:56.582 --> 00:33:57.690
'Cause if you could do that,

00:33:57.690 --> 00:33:59.800
imagine you could build
any tissue you wanted,

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:01.930
and he was trying lots of
different gene combinations

00:34:01.930 --> 00:34:04.610
and hit upon five genes
that when put together,

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:07.470
worked to take, so I could
take your skin cell now

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:10.050
using Yamanaka genes or Yamanaka factors

00:34:10.050 --> 00:34:12.980
and make a pluripotent stem
cell line and rebuild you.

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:15.100
I could even turn you, by the
way into a sperm and an egg,

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:16.744
and clone you, but I won't do that.

00:34:16.744 --> 00:34:18.610
So Yamanaka found the following genes.

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:23.130
So KLF4, SOX2, c-MYC, and LIN28.

00:34:23.130 --> 00:34:25.450
We use three of them
particularly in my lab,

00:34:25.450 --> 00:34:27.878
we'll talk about that later,
O, S, and K for short,

00:34:27.878 --> 00:34:29.500
but this was a major breakthrough,

00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:32.150
because finally you could
take anybody's skin cell,

00:34:32.150 --> 00:34:35.172
which you could easily get
from the cheek or a biopsy,

00:34:35.172 --> 00:34:38.750
grow that in a dish, reprogram them,

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:40.597
which takes about a week
once you put these genes in,

00:34:40.597 --> 00:34:42.880
and when they grow up into
what we call a colony,

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:44.920
which is an assemblage
of about a hundred cells,

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:46.789
you pick those, and now you could grow

00:34:46.789 --> 00:34:51.030
basically anything you
want from plant cells.

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:52.540
- Well, and I've seen this in your lab

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:56.257
where you've taken cells from a mouse,

00:34:56.257 --> 00:34:58.744
you've induced them into
a state of pluripotency,

00:34:58.744 --> 00:35:03.320
and then you have trained them,
for lack of a better word,

00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:06.248
to grow into brain cells.

00:35:06.248 --> 00:35:09.340
And now you have these little
clusters of brain cells

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:11.181
that look like little brains.

00:35:11.181 --> 00:35:12.700
- Well, we've done that for mice,

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:13.850
and we've done that for human.

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:15.100
We have many human brains,

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:18.080
and we take these from old individuals,

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:20.070
old normal individuals, and also patients

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:22.700
that have susceptibility
to Alzheimer's disease.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:26.007
And again, I think we're going
to talk about this later,

00:35:26.007 --> 00:35:29.960
but we have the ability in my
lab to drive aging forwards

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:32.239
in those what are called
cerebral organoids

00:35:32.239 --> 00:35:33.880
and even in reverse.

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:35.710
And that's what's important really

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:37.383
for being able to study the human brain

00:35:37.383 --> 00:35:40.030
without having to touch patients.

00:35:40.030 --> 00:35:41.780
And we can go a lot quicker that way.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:44.443
- Before we sort of go
down that rabbit hole,

00:35:44.443 --> 00:35:47.300
can I take us back just a couple of steps

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:50.360
to how this actually happens?

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:55.360
Like how do you take these
factors and put them into a cell?

00:35:56.777 --> 00:35:58.020
- Well, it's actually really easy.

00:35:58.020 --> 00:35:59.674
Any high school student could do it.

00:35:59.674 --> 00:36:01.535
There are a number of ways.

00:36:01.535 --> 00:36:04.970
The old ways of doing it
are you can pass electricity

00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:07.711
through the cells in the
dish and they take up DNA

00:36:07.711 --> 00:36:10.360
that encodes these Yamanaka genes.

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:13.060
You can put them in what are
called liposomes or liposomes,

00:36:13.060 --> 00:36:16.567
which are fat membrane bound little balls,

00:36:16.567 --> 00:36:18.590
but the way we do it in the lab,

00:36:18.590 --> 00:36:20.840
which is the most efficient,
is we use viruses,

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:24.110
domesticated viruses, that
are typically used in patients

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:26.120
for gene therapy, we use them in the lab,

00:36:26.120 --> 00:36:29.330
they called adeno-associated
viruses, or AAVs,

00:36:29.330 --> 00:36:31.495
we package inside those
the Yamanaka genes,

00:36:31.495 --> 00:36:33.430
and just throw them into the dish,

00:36:33.430 --> 00:36:35.650
and they infect all the cells in the dish,

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:38.580
and now those cells are
transgenic, they carry these genes,

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:40.700
and we can turn those
genes on and off at will

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:42.730
using drugs and see what happens.

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:45.310
- And the implication
here is that we can take

00:36:45.310 --> 00:36:50.310
these now pluripotent cells,
and then we can use them

00:36:50.312 --> 00:36:53.860
to treat areas of the body where the cells

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:55.610
just aren't acting the way

00:36:55.610 --> 00:36:57.500
that they're supposed to act anymore.

00:36:57.500 --> 00:37:01.290
But there's still a problem
with pluripotent cells, right?

00:37:01.290 --> 00:37:03.764
Or sorry, induced
pluripotent cells right now,

00:37:03.764 --> 00:37:06.400
and that is cancer.

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:08.540
- Yeah, if you take the
age of a cell back to zero

00:37:08.540 --> 00:37:11.190
and allow it to just grow and
if you put it into a mouse

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:13.560
and probably a human, you'll get cancer.

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:17.050
Teratoma's particularly
pernicious type of cancer,

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:20.750
which can be, you know,
a hairy ball of mass,

00:37:20.750 --> 00:37:22.350
sometimes with teeth in it.

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:23.420
You don't want that.

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:25.369
So you have to be very careful not to put

00:37:25.369 --> 00:37:28.180
these pluripotent stem
cells back into the body

00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:29.207
and just let them grow.

00:37:29.207 --> 00:37:32.466
- Which is why right now,
most stem cell therapies

00:37:32.466 --> 00:37:36.210
aren't using induced
pluripotent stem cells,

00:37:36.210 --> 00:37:38.150
they're using harvested stem cells,

00:37:38.150 --> 00:37:40.210
and we can get those
from a variety of places.

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:41.043
- Right.

00:37:41.043 --> 00:37:44.300
Mainly what we're using, or
what scientists and doctors

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:46.610
are using are what are
called autologous stem cells,

00:37:46.610 --> 00:37:48.850
so cells that you take out of your body

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:50.070
from various parts of the body,

00:37:50.070 --> 00:37:53.131
typically fat cells on
your back or your hips,

00:37:53.131 --> 00:37:56.170
and then purifying them
out, freezing them down

00:37:56.170 --> 00:37:57.720
and or putting them back into the body

00:37:57.720 --> 00:37:59.700
in different places or
into the bloodstream.

00:37:59.700 --> 00:38:02.710
- So we can just suck
stem cells out of fat,

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:04.044
out of adipose tissue.

00:38:04.044 --> 00:38:05.790
A lot of people think like,

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:07.628
well, why would we even
go through the process

00:38:07.628 --> 00:38:09.730
of trying to induce pluripotency

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:11.470
if we can just take stem cells out of fat?

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:14.150
But one of the things that
I think needs to be said,

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:15.023
and you've done some research on this,

00:38:15.023 --> 00:38:18.860
is that stem cells age also.

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:19.693
- They do.

00:38:19.693 --> 00:38:21.423
They undergo epigenetic
changes that make them

00:38:21.423 --> 00:38:24.800
less able to regenerate new tissues.

00:38:24.800 --> 00:38:26.270
And that's also true.

00:38:26.270 --> 00:38:28.800
You see that with gray hair and hair loss.

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:31.261
That's the loss of the stem
cells in the hair follicle.

00:38:31.261 --> 00:38:33.710
One other thing that
just happened recently,

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:35.280
was discovered that you don't just have

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:38.070
stem cells getting old,
they can even be kicked out

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:39.880
of what's called the
niche, where they belong,

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:42.033
they like to reside, and
that's true for the hair cell.

00:38:42.033 --> 00:38:45.854
There's a fascinating video
that I threw up on Twitter

00:38:45.854 --> 00:38:47.700
and the stem cell, you could see,

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:48.890
they captured it under the microscope,

00:38:48.890 --> 00:38:50.020
which got kicked out.

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:52.710
Anyway, suffice to say,
what is really going on

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:56.440
is that the stem cells
need to be a slowed down

00:38:56.440 --> 00:38:58.210
in their aging process or even replaced,

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:00.230
and we're only just
learning how to do that.

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:02.505
- The other thing that
we can do is capture them

00:39:02.505 --> 00:39:04.710
at a much younger state.

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:07.410
That's where cord blood comes in.

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:09.908
- Right, and you can
even do that for adults.

00:39:09.908 --> 00:39:12.300
The idea is that if
you capture these cells

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:13.270
when they're very young,

00:39:13.270 --> 00:39:15.260
or if you're in your
twenties and thirties,

00:39:15.260 --> 00:39:17.120
you can bank them, put them in a freezer

00:39:17.120 --> 00:39:20.610
for later use either to replace the blood

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:23.900
if you have leukemia, or
even using new technologies

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:26.764
to modify them and grow new tissues,

00:39:26.764 --> 00:39:30.440
perhaps even replacing old
blood when we get very old.

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:32.223
- A lot of people that are doing this now,

00:39:32.223 --> 00:39:35.757
it's becoming more and more
common for people to say,

00:39:35.757 --> 00:39:38.929
"hey, look, I want to
preserve my child's ability

00:39:38.929 --> 00:39:41.560
to use those stem cells later on,

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:43.180
even if we're not exactly sure

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:45.926
how they're going to be used,
and so they're banking them.

00:39:45.926 --> 00:39:49.912
Your kids are, in their
teens and early adult years,

00:39:49.912 --> 00:39:53.210
but if you could go back
in time, would you do this?

00:39:53.210 --> 00:39:54.840
- Yeah, we didn't do
that because at the time

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:56.220
it wasn't clear if they would be useful,

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:58.980
but now it's very clear that
there are uses for them,

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:01.180
especially if they get childhood cancer.

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:02.170
And so I would do that.

00:40:02.170 --> 00:40:04.780
If I was a new parent, I
would aim to bank those.

00:40:04.780 --> 00:40:05.770
How about you?

00:40:05.770 --> 00:40:07.270
- Yeah. I mean, so as you know,

00:40:07.270 --> 00:40:10.660
I haven't followed you down
every longevity rabbit hole

00:40:10.660 --> 00:40:12.880
that you've gone down and the
other people have gone down,

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:15.040
but this is definitely 100% something

00:40:15.040 --> 00:40:18.640
that if my child had been born
today, I would absolutely do.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:21.020
I would be banking their cord blood.

00:40:21.020 --> 00:40:21.853
- Yeah.

00:40:21.853 --> 00:40:23.150
- If I did want to do that now, though,

00:40:23.150 --> 00:40:26.090
how would I go about doing that?

00:40:26.090 --> 00:40:27.560
- Well, there are
companies you can contact

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:30.380
and often the hospital and
the doctors you work with

00:40:30.380 --> 00:40:32.690
know how to do this, but
you need to get ready,

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:34.440
you at least need a few days, often a week

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:36.770
to get ready for this, because
you need to spin them down

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:38.493
and put them in a freezer.

00:40:38.493 --> 00:40:40.420
But when you have a placenta,

00:40:40.420 --> 00:40:41.940
there are particular parts of the placenta

00:40:41.940 --> 00:40:43.415
you can take the cord blood,

00:40:43.415 --> 00:40:46.420
you can take a thing
called Wharton's jelly,

00:40:46.420 --> 00:40:49.820
which is this gelatinous
substance made of hyaluronic acid,

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:51.677
where there's particular
concentration of stem cells

00:40:51.677 --> 00:40:53.450
you can also isolate.

00:40:53.450 --> 00:40:56.650
- We can also isolate
stem cells from adults.

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:58.850
Again, these are stem
cells that have, you know,

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:01.454
gone down their trajectory
from pluripotency

00:41:01.454 --> 00:41:04.662
often to multipotency, and so they're not,

00:41:04.662 --> 00:41:05.990
you know, they're more aged,

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:08.546
but sort of the earlier we
do this, the better, right?

00:41:08.546 --> 00:41:10.869
- Yeah, these stem cells
in adults don't just age

00:41:10.869 --> 00:41:14.109
and become dysfunctional, they
can out-compete each other.

00:41:14.109 --> 00:41:15.710
They can grow faster.

00:41:15.710 --> 00:41:20.120
If you have mutations in
genes like Tet-1, Tet-2, DMTs,

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:22.150
they grow faster and
can actually out-compete

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:23.590
the rest of the immune system.

00:41:23.590 --> 00:41:25.050
If you look at hundred year olds,

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:26.370
often they only have one or two

00:41:26.370 --> 00:41:28.650
instead of thousands of
types of immune cells,

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:30.720
which is a real problem
if they get pneumonia.

00:41:30.720 --> 00:41:32.250
It's often why these elderly people

00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:33.870
don't survive in infection.

00:41:33.870 --> 00:41:37.786
So what you might want to
do is to bank these HSEs,

00:41:37.786 --> 00:41:39.670
hematopoietic stem cells
when you're younger,

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:41.650
so that you can replace your immune system

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:42.483
as you get older.

00:41:42.483 --> 00:41:45.787
- However we obtain the stem cells,

00:41:45.787 --> 00:41:50.787
researchers are using them
for all kinds of diseases.

00:41:51.580 --> 00:41:55.280
They're being used to
regenerate and repair disease

00:41:55.280 --> 00:41:58.113
in damaged tissues, in people
with spinal cord injuries,

00:41:58.113 --> 00:42:01.430
type one diabetes, Parkinson's disease,

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:03.888
ALS, Alzheimer's, heart disease,

00:42:03.888 --> 00:42:06.590
stroke, burns, cancer, arthritis,

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:08.336
and the reason why I'm
like making this big list

00:42:08.336 --> 00:42:11.040
is because now thanks to what,

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:12.510
I mean, like really
you've done this to me,

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:14.328
anytime I hear a big list of diseases,

00:42:14.328 --> 00:42:15.861
and one singular thing

00:42:15.861 --> 00:42:18.750
that can address all of those diseases,

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:21.050
I don't think of those
diseases separately anymore.

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:22.581
I think of aging.

00:42:22.581 --> 00:42:25.010
And there's a lot of implications here

00:42:25.010 --> 00:42:29.480
for the potential to use stem
cells perhaps to treat aging

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:31.380
and a lot of people
like these other things

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:32.290
we were talking about,

00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:35.673
are really excited about
this potential avenue

00:42:35.673 --> 00:42:37.090
for anti-aging.

00:42:37.090 --> 00:42:38.398
- Well, they do have a lot of potential.

00:42:38.398 --> 00:42:40.150
First of all, we can make new organs

00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:43.480
using these induced
pluripotent stem cells or IPCs.

00:42:43.480 --> 00:42:46.350
And even these autologous
stem cells we're talking about

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:49.110
that you can pull out from
children and from adults,

00:42:49.110 --> 00:42:52.710
they can be used to replace
stem cells that get old.

00:42:52.710 --> 00:42:54.170
But there's a real fundamental drawback

00:42:54.170 --> 00:42:56.267
to using stem cells to live longer.

00:42:56.267 --> 00:42:58.780
And that is that they often
don't find their niche,

00:42:58.780 --> 00:43:00.520
their resting place.

00:43:00.520 --> 00:43:02.100
Even if you inject them into the eye,

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:03.815
often they don't find their home,

00:43:03.815 --> 00:43:05.970
and in fact, one of the problems was

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:09.490
when researchers did this
to patients in Florida,

00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:10.870
there were three people who became blind

00:43:10.870 --> 00:43:12.270
when it wasn't done correctly.

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:13.103
So-

00:43:13.103 --> 00:43:15.130
- That coincided with an FDA order

00:43:15.130 --> 00:43:18.363
that said, "hey, everybody, slow it down.

00:43:18.363 --> 00:43:19.690
Be careful."

00:43:19.690 --> 00:43:21.920
- You can get some stem
cell therapies now,

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:25.186
but the majority of them
have been banned for now.

00:43:25.186 --> 00:43:27.950
But what is really a problem is that

00:43:27.950 --> 00:43:29.640
if you can't get them to the right place,

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:32.746
they're not going to
rejuvenate the whole body.

00:43:32.746 --> 00:43:34.360
So what we're looking at now is,

00:43:34.360 --> 00:43:36.320
how do you rejuvenate
the existing stem cells

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:37.290
and stop them getting old,

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:40.040
or even reverse the aging
of the stem cells, in situ,

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:42.080
where they exist, and we'll get to that,

00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:44.510
but this is the reprogramming
technology that should work,

00:43:44.510 --> 00:43:46.020
not just on normal cells,

00:43:46.020 --> 00:43:49.285
but even stem cells to make
them young, truly young again.

00:43:49.285 --> 00:43:51.176
- All right, and there's been
a lot of research showing

00:43:51.176 --> 00:43:54.291
that stem cells can have
a therapeutic effect

00:43:54.291 --> 00:43:56.160
in like specific parts of the body,

00:43:56.160 --> 00:43:59.080
but when we talk about this
like aging umbrella in general,

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:02.060
there was one study, actually two studies,

00:44:02.060 --> 00:44:04.730
a phase one study to phase
two study from researchers

00:44:04.730 --> 00:44:07.240
at the University of Miami in 2017

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:11.990
that gave stem cell therapy,
harvested stem cells

00:44:11.990 --> 00:44:16.990
from younger donors, gave
them to older frail patients

00:44:17.300 --> 00:44:22.300
and small scale, but
it showed improvements

00:44:22.733 --> 00:44:25.552
in the distances that
these people could walk,

00:44:25.552 --> 00:44:28.450
it lowered the levels of cytokines,

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:30.570
it improved their mental state,

00:44:30.570 --> 00:44:33.630
and they had a reported quality
of life improvement as well.

00:44:33.630 --> 00:44:35.123
- Yeah, I mean, that's remarkable.

00:44:35.123 --> 00:44:37.470
Couple of studies can be reproduced,

00:44:37.470 --> 00:44:39.720
but for now what most people are using

00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:42.922
the mesenchymal stem
cells for is in joints

00:44:42.922 --> 00:44:44.600
when you wear out the joint

00:44:44.600 --> 00:44:46.300
and there's not a lot of collagen,

00:44:46.300 --> 00:44:48.160
injections of stem cells into those joints

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:51.337
seems to be at least therapeutic
for a short amount of time,

00:44:51.337 --> 00:44:54.230
whether or not it's because
the stem cells stay in there

00:44:54.230 --> 00:44:57.500
or the secreting exosomes or
other peptides, it's unclear.

00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:01.940
- So this is another area
of research then that again,

00:45:01.940 --> 00:45:03.290
probably people don't want to

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:05.190
just walk into a clinic tomorrow

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:06.783
and get a stem cell injection.

00:45:06.783 --> 00:45:07.616
Because like you said,

00:45:07.616 --> 00:45:09.860
the stem cells might not
even know where to go,

00:45:09.860 --> 00:45:12.360
but in the future,

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:15.590
this is likely to be part of

00:45:15.590 --> 00:45:18.070
people's common anti-aging regimen.

00:45:18.070 --> 00:45:18.903
- Well, I'm sure.

00:45:18.903 --> 00:45:19.910
I think the future looks like this,

00:45:19.910 --> 00:45:22.483
that we can keep our bodies
healthy by eating right,

00:45:22.483 --> 00:45:24.090
doing physical exercise,

00:45:24.090 --> 00:45:26.740
taking the right medicine
slash supplements,

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:28.733
and when that doesn't
work and things fail,

00:45:28.733 --> 00:45:30.730
you can then rebuild the body,

00:45:30.730 --> 00:45:33.000
replace cells, put in your organs.

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:35.341
And that way, we'll live
probably many decades

00:45:35.341 --> 00:45:37.271
longer than we can currently live.

00:45:37.271 --> 00:45:40.190
- And then there's one more
thing that in the future

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:41.100
we may be able to do,

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:43.254
and this leads us to this thing

00:45:43.254 --> 00:45:46.100
that you're doing in your lab right now,

00:45:46.100 --> 00:45:48.576
you've had quite a few
published studies on

00:45:48.576 --> 00:45:51.341
that are pretty darn exciting.

00:45:51.341 --> 00:45:53.682
We're talking about
cellular reprogramming.

00:45:53.682 --> 00:45:55.233
- Well, this is the big one.

00:45:55.233 --> 00:45:58.635
We've been working for many
years on slowing aging,

00:45:58.635 --> 00:46:02.130
but we've wanted, how do
you get that to be reversed?

00:46:02.130 --> 00:46:05.090
Is there a reset switch in an old cell?

00:46:05.090 --> 00:46:06.740
And we think we found it.

00:46:06.740 --> 00:46:09.180
We're standing, of
course, on the shoulders

00:46:09.180 --> 00:46:11.800
of Shinya Yamanaka who
showed you can reset

00:46:11.800 --> 00:46:13.200
the age of a cell back to zero,

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:14.870
but that of course causes cancer,

00:46:14.870 --> 00:46:16.870
and if you do that in a
mouse, it'll die within days.

00:46:16.870 --> 00:46:18.680
So that's not going to be
a therapy anytime soon.

00:46:18.680 --> 00:46:19.775
I don't recommend it.

00:46:19.775 --> 00:46:24.060
But what a wonderful
student, Wan Chang Lu did

00:46:24.060 --> 00:46:26.444
a few years ago in my lab
was he found a set of genes

00:46:26.444 --> 00:46:30.850
that are Yamanaka factors
that were able to reverse

00:46:30.850 --> 00:46:33.600
the age of cells and tissues in an animal

00:46:33.600 --> 00:46:35.840
and in human cells to a certain point,

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:39.120
going back about 80% of
age, but not to zero.

00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:42.703
So that this was a new, safe
approach to reversing the age

00:46:42.703 --> 00:46:45.670
of the body without any
negative side effects.

00:46:45.670 --> 00:46:47.250
- And he did this is by instead of

00:46:47.250 --> 00:46:49.545
using all five Yamanaka
factors, you use three of them.

00:46:49.545 --> 00:46:52.241
- Three, and the c-MYC, which is the M,

00:46:52.241 --> 00:46:54.960
we definitely don't want
that in there, or LIN28.

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:57.370
These are what are called oncogenic genes,

00:46:57.370 --> 00:46:58.370
cancer causing genes.

00:46:58.370 --> 00:47:01.450
So we left those out, and
surprisingly those other three,

00:47:01.450 --> 00:47:03.268
O, S, and K for short, worked.

00:47:03.268 --> 00:47:07.093
Not just in cells in the dish,
but even in the whole animal.

00:47:07.093 --> 00:47:10.390
We focused on the eye because
we thought reversing blindness

00:47:10.390 --> 00:47:12.150
would be pretty cool and could be a drug,

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:14.910
a very quick drug to develop
relative to the whole body.

00:47:14.910 --> 00:47:15.743
And it worked.

00:47:15.743 --> 00:47:18.369
Wan Chang sent me a text
of a picture of an eye

00:47:18.369 --> 00:47:22.460
where he also regenerated the
optic nerve that was crushed,

00:47:22.460 --> 00:47:24.270
and then he went on to
restore vision in mice

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:25.330
that were given glaucoma,

00:47:25.330 --> 00:47:26.620
which is pressure in the eye,

00:47:26.620 --> 00:47:30.034
and even restored eyesight
to blind old mice.

00:47:30.034 --> 00:47:31.940
- Let's slow this down a little bit

00:47:31.940 --> 00:47:32.973
and take people through the process,

00:47:32.973 --> 00:47:35.460
because if we've gone from like, you know,

00:47:35.460 --> 00:47:38.570
Yamanaka factors to
restoring eyesight in mice.

00:47:38.570 --> 00:47:39.438
Let's talk about first,

00:47:39.438 --> 00:47:44.438
the conditions that
were trying to be fixed

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:47.765
were damaged optic nerve cells.

00:47:47.765 --> 00:47:52.130
And you used AAVs, these delivery viruses,

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:56.232
to send the three Yamanaka
factors into the cell.

00:47:56.232 --> 00:47:58.614
Once they were there,
what started happening?

00:47:58.614 --> 00:48:00.320
- Well, to be clear, our genome

00:48:00.320 --> 00:48:01.689
has the Yamanaka genes in it,

00:48:01.689 --> 00:48:03.560
but they're normally only turned on

00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:04.842
when we're little embryos.

00:48:04.842 --> 00:48:07.520
So we had to reintroduce those three genes

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:09.920
with a system where we
could turn them on at will,

00:48:09.920 --> 00:48:11.737
and we were using an
antibiotic to turn them on

00:48:11.737 --> 00:48:13.314
called doxycycline.

00:48:13.314 --> 00:48:16.392
We injected them with
the virus into the eye.

00:48:16.392 --> 00:48:18.300
It's a very simple procedure.

00:48:18.300 --> 00:48:19.810
In fact, a lot of patients that are blind

00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:22.600
get this treatment
already with other drugs.

00:48:22.600 --> 00:48:23.700
So it doesn't hurt.

00:48:23.700 --> 00:48:24.789
Only takes 10 minutes.

00:48:24.789 --> 00:48:26.601
We put into the eye of the mouse.

00:48:26.601 --> 00:48:28.740
We damage the eye and asked,

00:48:28.740 --> 00:48:32.010
could those reprogrammed
eyes that we reprogrammed

00:48:32.010 --> 00:48:33.670
for a month, could they regrow?

00:48:33.670 --> 00:48:34.790
And the answer was yes.

00:48:34.790 --> 00:48:37.210
- And not only did they regrow,

00:48:37.210 --> 00:48:39.420
but these mice that
were effectively blind,

00:48:39.420 --> 00:48:42.120
they were blind, you can do little tests

00:48:42.120 --> 00:48:44.140
and you can see that
their eyes actually work

00:48:44.140 --> 00:48:45.903
like young mice eyes again.

00:48:45.903 --> 00:48:49.610
- We put a pressure in
the eye to mimic glaucoma,

00:48:49.610 --> 00:48:51.548
which is a major cause of
blindness in the world,

00:48:51.548 --> 00:48:54.097
or we just let the mice
age out to one year of age

00:48:54.097 --> 00:48:55.970
and they were blind essentially.

00:48:55.970 --> 00:48:57.290
And we could tell that they were blind

00:48:57.290 --> 00:48:59.100
because we show them
little images that move,

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:01.040
lines actually that
moved from left to right.

00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:02.640
And if a mouse can see that,

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:03.860
young mice will move their heads,

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:05.400
it's called the opto-motor response.

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:06.580
- Literally, you can see videos of this.

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:07.602
It's really cute.

00:49:07.602 --> 00:49:08.590
They're like watching a tennis match.

00:49:08.590 --> 00:49:09.578
- Yeah, we do that too by the way.

00:49:09.578 --> 00:49:12.610
We also, of course, we measure
the electrical activity

00:49:12.610 --> 00:49:13.443
of those nerves.

00:49:13.443 --> 00:49:15.140
We also measure the age
of those nerve cells,

00:49:15.140 --> 00:49:17.220
we look at which genes
are switched on and off,

00:49:17.220 --> 00:49:20.570
and the conclusion was that
the nerves went back in time,

00:49:20.570 --> 00:49:24.860
back to the reset, full reset
to about 80%, their genes,

00:49:24.860 --> 00:49:26.450
the gene expression
patterns as we call it,

00:49:26.450 --> 00:49:27.920
which is which genes are on and off,

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:29.404
was reset to being young again,

00:49:29.404 --> 00:49:31.320
and the mice got their vision back,

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:34.430
as almost as good as they
were when they were young.

00:49:34.430 --> 00:49:36.127
- So a lot of people
will hear this and say,

00:49:36.127 --> 00:49:37.340
"man, this is really cool.

00:49:37.340 --> 00:49:39.135
You can reset blindness in a mouse

00:49:39.135 --> 00:49:41.060
or reset vision in a mouse.

00:49:41.060 --> 00:49:44.710
Can we do this with other cells?"

00:49:44.710 --> 00:49:46.680
- Yeah, it turns out that
it's not just nerve cells

00:49:46.680 --> 00:49:49.240
that can be rejuvenated
and sent back in time.

00:49:49.240 --> 00:49:50.600
We've done this for skin cells,

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:51.795
we're doing it for muscle cells,

00:49:51.795 --> 00:49:54.490
we've done it for other parts of the eye,

00:49:54.490 --> 00:49:55.820
and in fact that the shape of the eye

00:49:55.820 --> 00:49:57.030
and the structure of the eye,

00:49:57.030 --> 00:49:58.330
it doesn't just go back in time,

00:49:58.330 --> 00:50:01.320
it actually rebuilds itself
like it's young again.

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:03.540
So we're super optimistic that this may be

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:06.720
a universal mechanism of
age resetting in the body

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:09.078
that can not just be applied
to different mouse tissues,

00:50:09.078 --> 00:50:11.400
but also in humans as well.

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:13.383
And we're in the process,
hopefully in a year,

00:50:13.383 --> 00:50:16.370
year and a half, of
trying to restore vision

00:50:16.370 --> 00:50:18.240
in human patients that
have lost their vision

00:50:18.240 --> 00:50:21.420
due to either glaucoma
or genetic diseases.

00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:24.890
- So this is happening
because it's accessing

00:50:24.890 --> 00:50:29.560
the backup copy of the original
state of the genetic code

00:50:29.560 --> 00:50:31.510
that the cells are
building themselves up by?

00:50:31.510 --> 00:50:33.200
- Well, the genetic code stays the same,

00:50:33.200 --> 00:50:35.933
the epigenetic code, which
is the reader of the DNA,

00:50:35.933 --> 00:50:40.430
which we talked about in
episode one, that gets reset.

00:50:40.430 --> 00:50:42.160
So those structures of DNA folds

00:50:42.160 --> 00:50:44.640
that have gone awry during aging,

00:50:44.640 --> 00:50:46.700
this exdifferentiation process

00:50:46.700 --> 00:50:48.434
where cells lose their identity,

00:50:48.434 --> 00:50:51.617
that gets reset back to youthfulness.

00:50:51.617 --> 00:50:54.720
But how that happens, how
do you get a loop of DNA

00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:56.390
to go back into a bundle,

00:50:56.390 --> 00:50:58.242
and a bundle to go back into a loop?

00:50:58.242 --> 00:50:59.870
We have no idea how that works.

00:50:59.870 --> 00:51:02.900
We know that there's a backup
copy of not the genome,

00:51:02.900 --> 00:51:05.580
but the epigenome, the
structures that read the DNA,

00:51:05.580 --> 00:51:07.810
similar to polishing the scratches on a CD

00:51:07.810 --> 00:51:10.038
with the analogy we talked
about in episode one,

00:51:10.038 --> 00:51:11.540
but how does the cell do that?

00:51:11.540 --> 00:51:14.934
How does it reestablish
that original software,

00:51:14.934 --> 00:51:17.630
and re-install the software in the cell?

00:51:17.630 --> 00:51:19.516
We're only just trying
to figure that out now.

00:51:19.516 --> 00:51:21.720
Eventually we will know how this works

00:51:21.720 --> 00:51:23.870
and maybe we'll even develop a pill

00:51:23.870 --> 00:51:26.360
instead of a gene therapy
that could reset the age,

00:51:26.360 --> 00:51:28.740
not just of the eye, but the whole body.

00:51:28.740 --> 00:51:29.680
And when that happens,

00:51:29.680 --> 00:51:31.240
suddenly it can be a very different world.

00:51:31.240 --> 00:51:33.430
But until then we'll work on vision,

00:51:33.430 --> 00:51:35.230
and then we'll see where we go from there.

00:51:35.230 --> 00:51:36.150
- Well, suffice it to say,

00:51:36.150 --> 00:51:38.818
there are several ways that we think

00:51:38.818 --> 00:51:43.260
that we can get the human body to do this,

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:46.793
to reset the epigenetic clock.

00:51:47.810 --> 00:51:50.396
We can do it through
cellular reprogramming,

00:51:50.396 --> 00:51:54.982
it looks like it's possible
to do through exosomes,

00:51:54.982 --> 00:51:57.619
and you just mentioned pills.

00:51:57.619 --> 00:52:00.610
And it does appear that it's possible

00:52:00.610 --> 00:52:03.770
that this is what Metformin
is contributing to,

00:52:03.770 --> 00:52:05.840
and some of the other
drugs and supplements

00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:08.842
that we talked about
in an earlier episode,

00:52:08.842 --> 00:52:11.497
but I mention Metformin
because a lot of people

00:52:11.497 --> 00:52:14.010
are going to say, "okay,
all of this stuff is great.

00:52:14.010 --> 00:52:15.130
You've told me a bunch of stuff

00:52:15.130 --> 00:52:19.300
that probably won't be
accessible to me anytime soon."

00:52:19.300 --> 00:52:22.785
There is a treatment regimen

00:52:22.785 --> 00:52:26.173
that is being tested out right now

00:52:26.173 --> 00:52:28.440
that starts with Metformin,

00:52:28.440 --> 00:52:31.158
but a couple of other elements as well.

00:52:31.158 --> 00:52:32.460
Let's talk about that.

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:34.460
- Yeah, this is my colleague, Greg Fahy,

00:52:34.460 --> 00:52:36.453
Dr. Greg Fahy, and he's been working on

00:52:36.453 --> 00:52:39.630
anti-aging or longevity
treatments for awhile,

00:52:39.630 --> 00:52:41.886
and he hit upon a triple treatment.

00:52:41.886 --> 00:52:43.719
He uses growth hormone,

00:52:43.719 --> 00:52:46.280
which is known to raise
blood sugar levels,

00:52:46.280 --> 00:52:47.583
we talked about that as a downside,

00:52:47.583 --> 00:52:51.080
but then he realized if
he could include DHEA,

00:52:51.080 --> 00:52:53.500
a hormone that is depleted during aging,

00:52:53.500 --> 00:52:55.460
as well as Metformin, he could mitigate

00:52:55.460 --> 00:52:57.680
the negative effects of growth hormone.

00:52:57.680 --> 00:52:59.633
And he gave that for 12
months to 10 healthy men

00:52:59.633 --> 00:53:04.040
that were aged 51 to
65, and lo and behold,

00:53:04.040 --> 00:53:07.065
when he looked at the
biological clock of those men,

00:53:07.065 --> 00:53:09.630
they went back two and a half years.

00:53:09.630 --> 00:53:12.293
Which may not sound like a
lot, but as I thought about it,

00:53:12.293 --> 00:53:14.930
if you could truly reverse your age

00:53:14.930 --> 00:53:18.320
every year by one year, that
means you're not going to age.

00:53:18.320 --> 00:53:19.993
- All right, something that confuses me

00:53:19.993 --> 00:53:24.270
about this combinatory therapy regimen

00:53:24.270 --> 00:53:29.270
that Fahey used in this small-scale trial

00:53:29.290 --> 00:53:32.874
is that it takes advantage
of human growth hormone,

00:53:32.874 --> 00:53:34.767
which earlier in this episode we said

00:53:34.767 --> 00:53:39.040
probably not a long-term
anti-aging benefit.

00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:42.743
So what was the idea, do
you think, behind using HGH?

00:53:42.743 --> 00:53:45.210
- Well, HGH had been shown previously

00:53:45.210 --> 00:53:46.680
to reconstitute the thymus.

00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:49.330
This is a little organ gland

00:53:49.330 --> 00:53:50.640
that's here in your chest

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:53.120
that is important for the immune system.

00:53:53.120 --> 00:53:55.760
This is where your T cells,
your immune cells come from,

00:53:55.760 --> 00:53:58.060
and growth hormone can stimulate
the regeneration of this

00:53:58.060 --> 00:53:59.495
as it degrades over time.

00:53:59.495 --> 00:54:01.982
I'm age 50, I probably have what's called

00:54:01.982 --> 00:54:05.010
a lot of thymic
degeneration and involution.

00:54:05.010 --> 00:54:07.470
It's a vestige of what it used to be.

00:54:07.470 --> 00:54:09.270
And growth hormone can stimulate that,

00:54:09.270 --> 00:54:11.077
but it comes with side
effects as we mentioned,

00:54:11.077 --> 00:54:15.660
not least of which is increase
in insulin insensitivity

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:18.100
or glucose intolerance.

00:54:18.100 --> 00:54:20.380
In other words, the cell
doesn't take up the glucose

00:54:20.380 --> 00:54:22.560
from the body when you have a
lot of growth hormone around.

00:54:22.560 --> 00:54:25.910
So he combined that with
two diabetes therapies,

00:54:25.910 --> 00:54:28.250
one is Metformin, which everybody knows,

00:54:28.250 --> 00:54:31.240
and Greg also showed
actually in an experiment

00:54:31.240 --> 00:54:32.665
that he did on himself that DHEA,

00:54:32.665 --> 00:54:35.970
this hormone that some
people take for longevity,

00:54:35.970 --> 00:54:37.760
can also control blood glucose levels.

00:54:37.760 --> 00:54:40.588
So that combination of
three gave the best effect.

00:54:40.588 --> 00:54:43.680
But I should say that measuring
the clock in the blood

00:54:43.680 --> 00:54:45.850
doesn't mean the whole
body is being rejuvenated.

00:54:45.850 --> 00:54:47.270
That's just one indicator.

00:54:47.270 --> 00:54:49.230
So what I'd like to see
Greg and his colleagues do

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:51.040
is to measure aging of other tissues

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:52.710
and measure it using different clocks,

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:55.582
not just the Horvath or epigenetic clock.

00:54:55.582 --> 00:54:57.380
- We talked in an earlier episode

00:54:57.380 --> 00:54:59.683
about the proteomics clock.

00:54:59.683 --> 00:55:00.960
- There's a variety of clocks.

00:55:00.960 --> 00:55:01.890
There's glycan age,

00:55:01.890 --> 00:55:03.770
which are sugars that
are attached to proteins

00:55:03.770 --> 00:55:06.530
that change over time, and
there's one called immuno age.

00:55:06.530 --> 00:55:08.278
You measure various changes
in the immune system.

00:55:08.278 --> 00:55:11.390
Altogether, these can be
used to get a better picture

00:55:11.390 --> 00:55:14.560
of whether Greg's treatment
is truly reversing aging

00:55:14.560 --> 00:55:16.050
or just some aspects of it.

00:55:16.050 --> 00:55:17.850
- But the really frustrating thing is,

00:55:17.850 --> 00:55:19.290
even with these clocks right now,

00:55:19.290 --> 00:55:22.460
we don't know what we
don't know, five years,

00:55:22.460 --> 00:55:23.870
10 years, 20 years down the line.

00:55:23.870 --> 00:55:25.840
So it's a really good indicator

00:55:25.840 --> 00:55:27.410
if we can get these clocks to reset,

00:55:27.410 --> 00:55:31.074
if we can get these clocks
to lower, like Greg did,

00:55:31.074 --> 00:55:32.866
at least in the short term.

00:55:32.866 --> 00:55:36.610
In the long-term, do we have
to wait for the longterm?

00:55:36.610 --> 00:55:37.710
- Well, it depends.

00:55:37.710 --> 00:55:39.980
I know that Greg's research is ongoing

00:55:39.980 --> 00:55:42.410
and that I was with him
a couple of months ago,

00:55:42.410 --> 00:55:44.433
and he told me that repeated treatments

00:55:44.433 --> 00:55:46.866
over more than a year actually reduce age

00:55:46.866 --> 00:55:49.577
based on this blood clock, even more so,

00:55:49.577 --> 00:55:52.720
and people are going back
by a decade apparently,

00:55:52.720 --> 00:55:53.900
so super interesting time.

00:55:53.900 --> 00:55:55.400
I would say the jury is still out

00:55:55.400 --> 00:55:57.570
whether this is truly reversing aging

00:55:57.570 --> 00:55:59.929
or just some aspects of blood aging,

00:55:59.929 --> 00:56:02.640
but it's a very interesting
time that we live in

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:06.250
where people are ostensibly
going back in time.

00:56:06.250 --> 00:56:08.570
- One of the commonalities, I think,

00:56:08.570 --> 00:56:11.690
in almost all of these things
that we've talked about today

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:15.190
is there are these early indications

00:56:15.190 --> 00:56:17.510
that something might have effects

00:56:17.510 --> 00:56:21.390
and people are jumping over
one another to get to them,

00:56:21.390 --> 00:56:24.910
and you know, I mean, some
people are going overseas,

00:56:24.910 --> 00:56:25.910
if they can't get it here,

00:56:25.910 --> 00:56:27.600
they're finding doctors
willing to do this,

00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:29.097
they're finding suppliers

00:56:29.097 --> 00:56:32.136
from wherever on the internet they can,

00:56:32.136 --> 00:56:35.800
and it's understandable, right?

00:56:35.800 --> 00:56:39.000
Because people are suddenly
awakened to this idea

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:41.640
that aging may not be something

00:56:41.640 --> 00:56:44.450
that we have to tolerate in the long-term.

00:56:44.450 --> 00:56:48.821
So they're eager, but there's still work

00:56:48.821 --> 00:56:50.114
that needs to be done.

00:56:50.114 --> 00:56:50.947
- There is.

00:56:50.947 --> 00:56:52.370
But what I think is really exciting

00:56:52.370 --> 00:56:55.990
is we wrote our book,
"Lifespan" in 2018, '19,

00:56:55.990 --> 00:56:58.570
and we envisage a future where people

00:56:58.570 --> 00:57:00.493
could turn their clock
back and be rejuvenated.

00:57:00.493 --> 00:57:03.060
It's only been a couple
of years since then,

00:57:03.060 --> 00:57:05.790
and there's been a huge
advance in the ability

00:57:05.790 --> 00:57:08.263
to reprogram the body, to measure it.

00:57:08.263 --> 00:57:10.550
These tests are now
available to the public

00:57:10.550 --> 00:57:14.202
to measure biological age
from blood and from spit.

00:57:14.202 --> 00:57:17.470
We're in an age where I would
say it's hard to even predict

00:57:17.470 --> 00:57:20.400
where we'll be in five,
especially in 10 years from now.

00:57:20.400 --> 00:57:23.690
- But if you're frustrated,
if you're really overeager,

00:57:23.690 --> 00:57:27.500
there are things I think
this needs to be said.

00:57:27.500 --> 00:57:29.330
Like you can take steps right now.

00:57:29.330 --> 00:57:32.237
You don't have to necessarily
be taking peptides

00:57:32.237 --> 00:57:36.197
and exosomes and you don't
have to hunt down Greg and say,

00:57:36.197 --> 00:57:39.797
"you know, like give me
this combinatory therapy!"

00:57:40.760 --> 00:57:44.630
Just slowing down the
biological clock right now,

00:57:44.630 --> 00:57:46.200
doing the things that we talked about

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:47.740
in the first few episodes,

00:57:47.740 --> 00:57:51.350
is going to buy you that
time for these therapies

00:57:51.350 --> 00:57:56.280
to mature to the place where
we know that they're safe,

00:57:56.280 --> 00:57:57.570
we know what they're going to do.

00:57:57.570 --> 00:57:58.403
- Right.

00:57:58.403 --> 00:58:01.022
- So stay young, stay alive,
because the future looks bright

00:58:01.022 --> 00:58:03.458
just with the current pace of medicine

00:58:03.458 --> 00:58:06.500
and medical technologies,
if you live an extra year,

00:58:06.500 --> 00:58:08.320
you get another three months of life.

00:58:08.320 --> 00:58:12.230
We could easily approach a
time where every year you live,

00:58:12.230 --> 00:58:13.480
you get another year of life.

00:58:13.480 --> 00:58:15.668
And that's when the world
becomes super interesting.

00:58:15.668 --> 00:58:18.030
- What are we talking about next episode?

00:58:18.030 --> 00:58:20.410
- We are talking about
aging on the outside.

00:58:20.410 --> 00:58:22.955
So we've talked a lot about
how to keep your insides young.

00:58:22.955 --> 00:58:25.910
Next episode, we can talk
about your skin, your hair,

00:58:25.910 --> 00:58:27.980
your nails, how to keep
those looking good,

00:58:27.980 --> 00:58:29.400
and it's not just superficial.

00:58:29.400 --> 00:58:30.320
It's really important.

00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:32.710
It's actually been shown
that if you look younger

00:58:32.710 --> 00:58:34.800
on the outside, you probably
young on the inside too.

00:58:34.800 --> 00:58:35.756
So stay tuned for that.

